115.01 REGULAR MEETINGS.
   Unless otherwise ordered by vote of City Council, meetings of Council shall
be held in the Council Chambers located at 8500 Civic Center Boulevard, on the
first and third Tuesdays of each month beginning at 7:00 p.m.
(Ord. 08-0-12. Passed 2-5-08.)
115.02 SPECIAL MEETINGS.
   Upon written request of the President of Council or any three (3) members of
Council, special meetings of the Council may be called. Written notice thereof
shall be given by the Clerk of Council by serving the same upon the President
of Council and each member personally, or by leaving said notice at his usual
place of residence at least 24 hours before the time specified for such
meeting. The President of Council and each member may waive such notice, and
any defect or irregularity in the service of such notice shall be conclusively
waived by attendance at such meeting. Only such matters as are set forth in
such notice shall be considered and acted upon.
(1969 Code 30.02)
115.03 PRESIDENT OF COUNCIL.
   The President of Council, or in his absence the vice-president of Council,
shall take the chair at the hour for the meeting of the Council, and shall call
the Council to order. A majority of all members elected or appointed must be
present to constitute a quorum to do business; but a smaller number may adjourn
from time to time, and shall have the power to compel the attendance of the
absent members.
(1969 Code 30.03)
115.04 ORDER OF BUSINESS.
   The order of business of the Council shall be as follows:
   (a)    Roll call.
   (b)    Approval of minutes.
   (c)    Persons before Council.
   (d)    Public hearings.
   (e)    Legislation before Council.
   (f)    Manager's reports.
   (g)    Administrative reports.
   (h)    Correspondence, Clerk's.
    (i)    Official commission and/or committee reports.
   (j)    Old business.
   (k)    New business.
   (l)    Announcements.
   (m)    Final hearing of persons before Council.
   (n)    Adjournment.
      (1969 Code 30.04)
115.05 CHANGE IN ORDER OF BUSINESS.
   The order of business may be changed by the presiding officer unless there
is an objection by a majority of Council. Before proceeding out of order, the
presiding officer shall inquire if there are any objections.
(1969 Code 30.05)
115.06 RECEIVING MOTION WHEN QUESTION IS BEFORE COUNCIL.
   When a question or a proposition is before Council or under debate, or when
a motion has been made, no motions shall be received except the following:
    (a)    To adjourn.
    (b)    To table the motion - lay it aside until later.
   (c)    To request that discussion end, and that any motion being considered
to be voted on. (Previous question, as it is usually called.)
    (d)    To postpone any action on a motion until some stated future time.
   (e)    To refer the proposal to a standing or special committee.
    (f)    To amend the main motion.
   (g)    To postpone any action for an indefinite time.
   These motions have precedence in the order in which they are arranged.
Motions listed as (a), (b) or (c) above shall be decided without debate.
(1969 Code 30.06)
115.07 MOTIONS TO RECONSIDER.
   A motion to reconsider a subject that may have been acted upon favorably
must be made before the adjournment of that session of Council. A motion to
reconsider any other subject may be brought not later than the next regular
meeting after such action was taken. A motion to reconsider shall be in order
at any time except when a motion on some other subject is standing.
   A motion to reconsider, being laid on the table, may be taken up and acted
upon at any time when Council is engaged in the transaction of new or
miscellaneous business.
   No motion to reconsider shall be made more than once on any matter or
subject, and the same number of votes shall be required to reconsider the
action of Council as was required to pass or adopt the same.
(1969 Code 30.07)
115.08 MOTION TO TAKE FROM TABLE.
   A motion to take from the table shall be in order when that order of
business is being transacted in which such matter to be taken-up was laid upon
the table, or under the head of new or miscellaneous business, and such motion
shall be decided without debate, provided the mover may be permitted to state
briefly his reasons for the motion.
(1969 Code 30.08)
115.09 MOTION TO POSTPONE INDEFINITELY.
   If a motion to postpone indefinitely be carried, the principal question
shall be declared lost. (1969 Code 30.09)
115.10 MOTION TO AMEND.
   A motion to amend is susceptible of but one amendment thereof. An amendment
once rejected may not be moved again in the same form.
(1969 Code 30.10)
115.11 AMENDING RESOLUTION OR ORDINANCE.
   It shall be in order to amend a resolution or ordinance at any time, but if
such ordinance or resolution be of a general or permanent nature, and such
amendment is made after the second reading, it shall again be read as amended,
as the second reading, and laid over to the next meeting for final action.
(1969 Code 30.11)
115.12 DIRECTOR OF LAW.
   The Director of Law shall, when requested by any member of Council, give a
verbal opinion on any question of law concerning City affairs, in open Council,
but the Law Director may, if the Law Director deems the matter of importance,
take a reasonable time to submit his or her opinion orally or in writing. He
shall not be required to draw any ordinance or resolution except upon a
majority vote of the members of Council, or the President of Council.
   The Law Director, when requested by the chairman of a committee to which any
ordinance or resolution has been referred, shall assist in the examination of
such ordinance or resolution.
(1969 Code 30.12)
115.13 PRESERVING ORDER.
   The presiding officer shall at all times preserve order and decorum. The
Presiding officer shall decide questions of order, subject to an appeal to the
Council by any member, on which appeal no member shall speak more than once.
(1969 Code 30.13)
115.14 CONTROL OF THE COUNCIL CHAMBER.
   The presiding officer shall have general direction and control of the
Council Chambers in case of any disturbance or disorderly conduct in the
Council Chambers or so near thereto as to disturb the orderly proceedings of
Council, and may cause the same to be cleared.
(1969 Code 30.14)
115.15 MEMBERS PRESIDING.
   The presiding officer may name any member to perform the duties of the chair
during any meeting at which the presiding officer is present.
(1969 Code 30.15)
115.16 SPECIAL COMMITTEES.
   Special committees for the consideration of special measures or matters, or
for the performance of special legislative functions, may be appointed by the
presiding officer.
(Ord. 08-0-12. Passed 2-5-08.)
115.17 QUORUM.
   A majority of all members of a committee shall constitute a quorum.
(Ord. 08-0-12. Passed 2-5-08.)
115.18 COMMITTEE MEETINGS.
   (a)   It shall be the duty of the chairman to give notice of all committee
meetings to members of the committee. Notice of meetings shall also be given to
the Clerk by the Chairman prior to 3:00PM the Thursday of the week preceding
the meeting. Public notice of said meeting, including the date, time and
location, shall be advertised on the City’s web site, the Mentor Channel and
shall be forwarded to a newspaper of local circulation. The Clerk shall also
include any such notices to all persons receiving Friday legislative packets
and/or agendas.
   The affirmative vote of a majority of all members constituting such
committee shall be necessary to report any recommendation with respect to any
subject referred to the committee. Three consecutive absences from committee
meetings shall operate to suspend the member from such committee, unless
excused by the remaining members thereof. All suspensions shall be reported to
Council forthwith. No committee or member thereof shall be permitted to incur
any expense without first receiving the consent of Council.
   (b)   It shall be the duty of the chairman, or a designee, to document the
proceedings of said committee meeting by completing a form that includes the
name of the committee, the date of meeting, the time of commencement and
adjournment, the persons present and a brief description of the matters
discussed. Any recommendation adopted pursuant to a committee meeting shall be
reflected fully in writing in the minutes with the yes or no votes of persons
voting thereon duly recorded. Said form shall constitute the minutes of the
meeting and shall be submitted to the Clerk at the end of the meeting and
maintained pursuant to duly adopted record retention schedules. The Clerk shall
also maintain printed forms for use in the documentation of committee meetings.
Information required by law to be kept confidential shall not be included in
the minutes.
(Ord. 08-0-12. Passed 2-5-08.)
115.19 MEMBERS DESIRING TO SPEAK.
   When any member of Council desires to address the body, the member shall
address the chair, saying, "Mr. President, or "Mr. Vice-President". The
presiding officer shall then recognize the member by name. In the event two
members desire to speak at the same time, the presiding officer shall name the
member who is to have the floor. No member shall speak more than twice on the
same question, except with permission of the Council. The member shall confine
himself or herself to the question under discussion or debate. The member shall
avoid personalities. No member shall speak upon one question, ordinance or
resolution, more than a total of ten (10) minutes at any meeting, unless
additional time be granted by a majority of the Council upon motion.
(1969 Code 30.l9)
115.20 TRANSGRESSION OF RULES.
   If any member, in speaking or otherwise, transgresses the rules of Council,
the presiding officer shall call the offending member to order. Any member may,
by raising the point of order, call the attention of the presiding officer to
such transgression, and the point of order shall be decided by the presiding
officer without debate.
(1969 Code 30.20)
115.21 REFERENCE TO COMMITTEES.
   Any legislative matter brought to the attention of Council may be referred
by the presiding officer to a standing or a special committee as will, in the
officer's judgment and discretion, be proper. All such matters shall be
investigated and report made to Council as a whole within such time as is fixed
by the presiding officer. A motion to discharge a committee of further
consideration of any matter referred to it shall be in order upon the
expiration of the time so fixed. Roll call shall be had on such motion and a
majority of the members of Council must vote favorably upon the motion before
the adoption thereof.
(1969 Code 30.21)
115.22 SERGEANT AT ARMS.
   The President of Council, or any two other members of Council may require
that at each regular or special meeting of Council, the Chief of Police, or
such officer or member of the Department of Police as the Chief shall designate
shall be in attendance and shall perform the duties ordinarily performed by a
sergeant at arms.
(1969 Code 30.23)
115.23 ROLL CALL OR VOTE OF COUNCIL.
   The Clerk of Council shall call the roll of members of Council, for any
purpose, in alphabetical order of their last names, unless otherwise directed
at any given meeting by a simple majority of Council present.
(1969 Code 30.24)
115.24 COUNCIL EXECUTIVE SESSIONS.
   All meetings and executive sessions of Council shall be public meetings or
sessions open to the public, provided however, that the Council may hold
executive sessions not open to the public for the following purposes:
(a)    To consider the appointment, employment, dismissal, discipline,
promotion, demotion, or compensation of an employee or official of the City of
Mentor; or the investigation of charges or complaints against an employee or
official of the City of Mentor. Council shall not however, hold an executive
session for the discipline of a member of Council for conduct relating to the
performance of the member's official duties or for the member's removal from
office.
   (b)    To consider the purchase of property for public purposes, or for the
sale of property at competitive bidding, if premature disclosure of information
would give an unfair competitive or bargaining advantage to a person whose
personal, private interest is adverse to the general public interest. No member
of Council shall use this section as a subterfuge for providing covert
information to prospective buyers or sellers.
   (c)    Conferences with the Law Director or the Director's assistants or
other legal counsel retained by the City concerning matters or disputes
involving the City of Mentor that are the subject of pending or imminent court
action.
   (d)    Preparing for, conducting, or reviewing negotiations or bargaining
sessions with employees of the City of Mentor concerning their compensation or
other terms and conditions of their employment.
   (e)    Matters required to be kept confidential by federal law or rules or
state statutes.
   (f)    Specialized details of security arrangements where disclosure of the
matters discussed might reveal information that could be used for the purpose
of committing, or avoiding prosecution for a violation of the law.
   Minutes of an executive session shall be taken by either the Clerk of
Council or a Council member and shall be open to public inspection, provided
however, that the minutes need only reflect the general subject matter of
discussions in the executive session.
(1969 Code 30.25)
115.25 NOTIFICATION OF COUNCIL MEETINGS; FEES.
   (a)    Persons and/or organizations desiring advance notifications of
regular and special meetings of Council shall receive same upon payment to the
City of Mentor of the sum of Fifteen dollars ($15.00) for each calendar year or
part thereof, which sum shall be paid in advance and shall be non-refundable.
   (b)    Persons and/or organizations complying with subsection (a) hereof
shall receive notice of all regular and special meetings of Council, and the
Clerk of Council shall maintain a listing of the names, addresses and telephone
numbers of all such persons and organizations.
   (c)    Notification pursuant hereto shall be in a manner as determined by
the Clerk of Council.
(1969 Code 30.26)
115.26 PERSONS BEFORE COUNCIL.
   Persons Before Council is defined as those parts of a regular meeting of
Council where persons address Council concerning issues affecting the City. No
speaker at Persons Before Council shall be permitted more than four minutes of
speaking time at any meeting unless interrupted. Issue groups and candidates
are also permitted to participate in Persons Before Council, but no signs,
buttons or other advertisements shall be displayed on or with any speaker
regardless of whether the speaker represents an issue, a candidate or
otherwise. No visual display shall be permitted through copy or electronic
format at Persons Before Council unless the display was filed with the City as
part of a rezoning or other application being heard by the Council pursuant to
the Mentor Code of Ordinances. Any display shall be delivered to the offices of
the Council President and the City Manager by 1:00 p.m. the business day
immediately preceding the meeting. In light of the government access
telecasting of Council meetings, no speech or display in Persons Before Council
shall be permitted to advertise for commercial gain or enterprise.
(Ord. 08-0-83. Passed 8-19-08.)
115.27 PUBLIC HEARINGS.
   No visual display shall be permitted through copy or electronic format
during a Public Hearing of Council unless the display was filed with the City
as part of a rezoning or other application being heard by the Council pursuant
to the Mentor Code of Ordinances. Any display shall be delivered to the offices
of the Council President and the City Manager by 1:00 p.m. the business day
immediately preceding the meeting. In light of the government access
telecasting of Council meetings, no speech or display during a Public Hearing
shall be permitted to advertise for commercial gain or enterprise.
(Ord. 08-0-85. Passed 8-19-08.)
115.28 POSTING COMMISSION/BOARD APPOINTMENTS.
   All terms of Commissions and Boards required by the City of Mentor Charter
or Code of Ordinances to be filled because of term expiration or vacancy shall
be conspicuously posted through the City of Mentor web site and through the
City of Mentor television channel as follows: for expiring terms, the posting
shall be from Monday of the week of the November general election preceding the
term expiration until December 15. In the event of a vacancy during a term, the
posting shall be for two weeks, commencing one week after receipt by the Clerk
of Council or the President of Council of the vacancy. Posting shall be in the
same manner as posting for beginning terms. Commission and Board members and
all other persons seeking (re)appointment to an expiring term must submit a
resume to the Clerk of Council no later than December 15, or in the event of a
vacancy during a term, all persons seeking appointment must submit a resume to
the Clerk of Council within the two week posting period. Resumes will then be
distributed to Council. (Ord. 09-0-93. Passed 9-1-09.)
115.29 COMMISSION/BOARD APPLICATIONS AND BACKGROUND CHECKS.
   Commission and Board members and all other persons seeking (re)appointment
to an expiring or vacant term, in order to be eligible, and in addition to the
requirements of MCO
115.28, must submit with a resume a completed City supplied Application for
Commission/Board Appointment/Reappointment form and an executed City supplied
consent form for a felony and misdemeanor criminal and civil judgement
background check.
(Ord. 10-0-12. Passed 3-2-10.)
CHAPTER 117
Ordinances and Resolutions
117.01    Action to be taken by ordinance or resolution.
117.02   Introduction of ordinances and resolutions.
117.03   Style of ordinances and resolutions.
117.04   Subject of ordinances and resolutions.
117.05   Emergency measures.
117.06   Reading of ordinances and resolutions of a general or permanent
nature.
117.07   Resolutions not of a general or permanent nature.
117.08   Effective date.
117.09   Votes required for passage of zoning change and land use change
legislation.
117.10   Public notice.
 
 
CROSS REFERENCES
Ordinances and resolutions - see CHTR.
3.09
Emergency ordinances and resolutions - see CHTR.
 3.10
Initiative and referendum - see CHTR.
9.01
 
 
 
117.01 ACTION TO BE TAKEN BY ORDINANCE OR RESOLUTION.
   Action of Council shall be by ordinance, resolution or motion. Every action
of a general and permanent nature, or granting a franchise; or levying a tax;
or appropriating money; or contracting an indebtedness, to be evidenced by the
issuance of bonds or notes; or for the purchase, lease or transfer of public
property; or establishing an offense and fixing the penalty therefor shall be
taken by ordinance, in the manner hereinafter provided. All other action may be
by resolution or motion. (1969 Code 3.01)
117.02 INTRODUCTION OF ORDINANCES AND RESOLUTIONS.
   Ordinances and resolutions shall be introduced at regularly convened Council
meetings only in written or printed form. (1969 Code 3.02)
117.03 STYLE OF ORDINANCES AND RESOLUTIONS.
   Each ordinance shall contain an enacting clause as follows: "Be It ordained
By The Council of The City of Mentor, State of Ohio", and each resolution shall
contain an enacting clause as follows: "Be It Resolved By The Council of The
City of Mentor, State of Ohio".
(1969 Code 3.03)
117.04 SUBJECT OF ORDINANCES AND RESOLUTIONS.
   Each ordinance or resolution shall contain only one subject which shall be
expressed and designated in its title in a concise manner, provided, however,
that appropriation ordinances may contain the various subjects, accounts, and
amounts for which monies are appropriated, and ordinances and resolutions
providing for a codification or recodification are not subject to the
limitation of containing one subject.
(1969 Code 3.04)
117.05 EMERGENCY MEASURES.
   No ordinance or resolution shall be passed or adopted as an emergency
measure unless it shall have been introduced as such and unless it shall set
forth in the final section thereof a declaration of the circumstances
constituting the emergency which exists, necessitating the immediate going into
effect of the ordinance or resolution in order to provide for the preservation
of the public peace, property, health, safety or welfare from an immediate
danger, or to provide for the usual daily operation of a department or an
office of the City.
(1969 Code 3.05)
117.06 READING OF ORDINANCES AND RESOLUTIONS OF A GENERAL OR PERMANENT NATURE.
   Each ordinance and resolution shall be introduced and read by title only,
provided that Council may require any reading to be in full by a majority vote
of its members.
   Each ordinance and each resolution of a general or permanent nature shall be
read by title on three different days, provided Council may dispense with this
rule by an affirmative vote of at least three-fourths of its members.
(1969 Code 3.06)
117.07 RESOLUTIONS NOT OF A GENERAL OR PERMANENT NATURE.
   Resolutions not of a general or permanent nature shall be introduced for
Council action by having it read by title only. Council may then take action on
such resolution and vote on it immediately. (1969 Code 3.07)
117.08 EFFECTIVE DATE.
   Upon the final reading of an ordinance or resolution, the same shall be
passed or adopted upon receiving an affirmative vote of four members of the
Council, unless the same be an emergency measure, in which event the
affirmative vote of at least five members of Council shall be required for this
enactment as an emergency measure. Emergency measures and ordinances described
in Ohio R.C. 731.30, become effective immediately upon signature by the
President of Council.
   Failure of the ordinance or resolution as an emergency measure shall only
affect the effective date of the ordinance or resolution, and it, as all other
passed or adopted ordinances and resolutions, shall take effect 30 days after
signature by the President of Council, except as otherwise provided in Section
3.09 of the Charter.
(1969 Code 3.08)
117.09 VOTES REQUIRED FOR PASSAGE OF ZONING CHANGE AND LAND USE CHANGE
LEGISLATION.
   (a)    Legislation relating to zoning changes and land use changes which has
been favorably recommended by the Planning Commission to Council as a result of
four or more members of the Planning Commission favoring same, shall require
only a majority vote of the Councilmen present at a duly constituted meeting,
for passage of said legislation.
   (b)    An affirmative vote of five members of Council shall be necessary for
passage of zoning change or land use change legislation if the favorable
recommendation received from the Planning Commission resulted from an
affirmative vote of less than four members of the Planning Commission.
   (c)    An affirmative vote of six members of Council shall be required for
the passage of zoning change and land use change legislation upon which the
Planning Commission has made an unfavorable recommendation to Council.
(1969 Code 3.081)
117.10 PUBLIC NOTICE.
   Public notice of ordinances and resolutions shall be given by publishing the
ordinance or resolution number, the title thereof and the date of passage or
adoption, except that, in lieu thereof, any ordinance or resolution may be
published in full or posted when so provided for therein. Publication of an
ordinance or resolution, whether by number, title and date of passage, or in
full, shall be made once a week for two consecutive weeks on the same day of
each week in a newspaper of general circulation in the City; and, in case of
posting, copies of the ordinance or resolution shall be posted for not less
than 15 days in not less than five of the most public places in the City as
heretofore or hereafter designated by ordinance.
(1969 Code 3.09)
CHAPTER 119
Democracy Day
119.01   Designation of Democracy Day.
119.01   Notifications.
119.03   Continuation of public hearings.
119.04   Effective date.
 
 
119.01 DESIGNATION OF DEMOCRACY DAY.
   Beginning in the year of 2015, the City Manager and City Council shall
designate one day in the month of February following the November Federal
elections, "Democracy Day." On this day, the City Manager and City Council
shall sponsor a Public Hearing in a public space within the City. The City
shall publicize the Public Hearing on its website and through area media at
least one (1) month in advance of the Hearing. The Public Hearing will examine
the impact of political contributions of corporations, unions, PACS and Super-
PACS on the City. The City Manager and at least one (1) City Councilperson
shall submit testimony at the Public Hearing. In addition, all citizens of
Mentor will be permitted to submit oral testimony for a period of at least five
(5) minutes per citizen.
(Ord. 15-O-07. Passed 1-20-15.)
119.02 NOTIFICATIONS.
   Within one (1) week following the Public Hearing, the City Manager shall
send a letter to the leaders of the Ohio House and Senate, and Mentor's US
Congressional representative, and both Ohio US Senators stating that the
citizens of Mentor in November 2014 voted in support of a Citizen's initiative
calling for a constitutional amendment declaring:
   (a)   Only human beings, not corporations, are legal persons with
constitutional rights;
   (b)   Money is not equivalent to speech, and therefore regulating political
contributions and spending is not equivalent to limiting political speech.
      (Ord. 15-O-07. Passed 1-20-15.)
119.03 CONTINUATION OF PUBLIC HEARINGS.
   The biennial Public Hearings will continue for a period of ten (10) years
through February, 2035 or until a constitutional amendment reflecting the
principles set forth in Section
119.02 is ratified by 3/4 of state legislatures.
(Ord. 25-O-023. Passed 3-4-25.)
119.04 EFFECTIVE DATE.
   This initiative shall take effect and be included in the City Ordinances at
the earliest date permitted under Section
117.08 of the Codified Ordinances of the City of Mentor, 2006, as amended.
(Ord. 15-O-07. Passed 1-20-15.)
