CHAPTER 121
City Manager
121.01   Manager to be Safety Director.
121.02    Payment of pre-employment records.
121.03    Schedule of charges for copies of Municipal documents and expenses.
121.04   Authorization to sell seized and/or forfeited vehicles.
121.05   Disposal of surplus and/or obsolete personal property.
121.06   Adoption of administrative fee schedules.
 
 
CROSS REFERENCES
General Charter provisions - see CHTR.
Art. IV
Contracting authority - see CHTR.
7.03
 
 
 
121.01 MANAGER TO BE SAFETY DIRECTOR.
   (a)   Pursuant to Section
5.01 of the Charter, the Manager is hereby authorized and required to serve as
Director of Safety, and shall perform the duties of said office until such time
as is otherwise determined by resolution of Council.
   (b)    From and after January 1, 1964, the duties of the City Manager shall
be confined to those of the office of the City Manager and the office of the
Director of Safety. From and after said date, the duties of the Director of
Finance shall be performed by a person other than the City Manager. (1969 Code
31.051)
   (c)   Duties; Contracts.
      (1)   From and after January 1, 2006, the duties of the Manager or his
designee shall be, prior to the awarding of competitively bid contracts, to
evaluate whether the apparent lowest bidder is also the lowest and best bidder
pursuant to Section
 7.03 of the Charter of the City of Mentor. The City Manager shall consider the
following criteria:
         A.   Whether the bid responds to bid specifications in all material
respects and contains no irregularities or deviations from the specifications
which would affect the amount of the bid or otherwise give the bidder a
competitive advantage.
         B.   Whether the bidder is experienced in providing the services
offered.
         C.   Whether the financial condition of the bidder is satisfactory.
         D.   Whether the conduct and performance of the bidder on previous
contracts has been satisfactory.
         E.   Whether the facilities available to and management skills of the
bidder are satisfactory.
         F.   Whether the bidder possesses the ability to execute the contract
properly.
The City Manager shall adopt a form for completion that requires bidders to
include the following types of information with a bid: the length of time the
bidder has been in business, whether the bidder has been rejected for a public
contract as low bidder because of not being responsible, whether the bidder has
had claims against or a performance bond cancelled, whether the bidder had paid
penalties imposed as a result of delay on a public project, whether the bidder
has been found to have committed an unfair labor practice or any other
employment/labor law violation in such areas as discrimination, prevailing
wage, Workers’ Compensation or OSHA, and also notifies the bidder of the
requirements in subsections (c)(2) and (c)(3). The City Manager shall recommend
for rejection any apparent lowest bidder that is not the lowest and best bidder
based on the information supplied, and he shall notify the apparent lowest
bidder of the reasons for the determination in writing by certified mail.
      (2)   If the City Manager receives a protest in writing within seven (7)
days of certified mail receipt by the apparent lowest bidder of the City
Manager’s determination, the City Manager shall conduct a hearing where the
apparent lowest bidder may present supplemental information and documentation
to change the determination of the City Manager. After hearing, the City
Manager shall either affirm or reverse the determination. Any additional
financial information delivered to the City Manager shall be kept confidential
and, pursuant to Ohio R.C. 9.312, shall not be considered a public record.
      (3)   Any bidder awarded a contract shall provide an affidavit to the
City Manager prior to final payment on the contract stating that it has
complied with all Federal, State and local taxation requirements including the
filing of all returns due and the payment of all taxes then payable and that to
its knowledge any labor performed pursuant to the contract was performed by
workers accurately classified pursuant to Internal Revenue Service guidelines
as either employees or independent contractors.
         (Ord. 06-0-07. Passed 2-21-06.)
121.02 PAYMENT OF PRE-EMPLOYMENT EXPENSES.
   Within funds appropriated by City Council for such purpose, the City Manager
may authorize payment of pre-employment expenses to applicants for
administrative and supervisory positions, including, but not limited to,
reimbursement of travel and lodging expenses of applicants being interviewed.
(1969 Code 31.71)
121.03 SCHEDULE OF CHARGES FOR COPIES OF MUNICIPAL DOCUMENTS AND RECORDS.
   The City Manager shall establish a schedule of charges for copies of
municipal documents and records, which schedule of charges and any revisions
thereof shall be on file with the Clerk of Council. (1969 Code 31.72)
121.04 AUTHORIZATION TO SELL SEIZED AND/OR FORFEITED VEHICLES.
   (a)   Council determines that it is in the best interest of the City to sell
seized and/or forfeited vehicles whenever the City obtains possession.
   (b)   The City Manager is hereby authorized to solicit bids and sell these
seized and forfeited vehicles on the open market, upon such terms and
conditions as are in the best interests of the City.
   (c)   The Public Internet Auction for the aforementioned vehicles shall be
held on the Public Surplus web site (www.publicsurplus.com) for a period of 15
days. Notice of this auction will also be advertised in the News Herald, on
Channel 12 and on the City web site in accordance with all applicable local,
state and federal laws.
   (d)   Any transaction pursuant to this section shall be conducted in
accordance with the following procedure:
      (1)   Prior to the open bidding period, notice of the City’s intent to
sell property via internet auction shall be published at least once a week for
three successive weeks in the News Herald. Said notice shall include a complete
description of the vehicles to be sold; the date, time and location where the
auction will be held; the terms and conditions of sale; and any other
information necessary to allow interested members of the general public to
participate in the online public auction.
      (2)   Prior to the commencement of the open bidding period, notice of the
City’s intent to sell property via Internet auction shall be posted in a
conspicuous place in City Hall.
      (3)   Prior to the commencement of the open bidding period, notice of the
City’s intent to sell property via Internet auction shall be published on the
City’s website at www.cityofmentor.com/living/citynews.php
      (4)   Each vehicle will be listed for open public bidding for a minimum
of fifteen (15) days, including weekends and legal holidays.
      (5)   Vehicles will be listed “for sale” for public auction on the Public
Surplus web site. The listing shall describe the vehicles in detail including
but not limited to the following items: the price, make, model, color, general
operating condition, condition of the exterior, condition of the interior, the
odometer reading of the vehicle, any other feature of the vehicle that would
interest the reasonable consumer. A picture of each vehicle for sale shall
accompany every listing.
      (6)   The terms of sale will be as follows: The highest bidder upon the
close of the open bidding period will be deemed the successful bidder at the
online auction. Upon the close of the auction, the City shall remit an invoice
to the successful bidder for the amount of the bid. The successful bidder shall
deposit five percent (5%) of the winning bid within five (5) days of the
vehicle auction’s close. Any funds remitted pursuant to this initial deposit
shall be nonrefundable. The remaining balance must be remitted within ten (10)
days of the vehicle auction’s close. No vehicle will be made available for
shipping to the purchaser prior to the purchaser’s obligation being satisfied
in full. Acceptable forms of payment include cashier’s check or money order.
      (7)   At the close of the sale, contact shall be made with the successful
bidder to discuss maters involving title transfer, payment and delivery of the
vehicle to the purchaser. Successful bidder will be responsible for the removal
of item(s). Successful bidder will be responsible for cost of shipping item(s)
if necessary. All communication with the successful bidder shall occur in
writing.
      (8)   Arrangements and costs associated with delivery of the subject
vehicle to the successful bidder shall be the sole responsibility of the
purchaser. Risk of loss will be transferred to the successful bidder upon the
tendering of the goods to the shipping company chosen by the purchaser.
      (9)   All vehicles shall be sold without warranty in “AS IS” condition.
All advertisements, listings and notices shall contain the following language:
THE CITY OF MENTOR, ITS OFFICERS, EMPLOYERS AND AGENTS OFFER THESE VEHICLES FOR
SALE “AS IS” WITHOUT ANY WARRANTY OR CONDITION, EXPRESS, IMPLIED OR STATUTORY.
THE CITY OF MENTOR, ITS OFFICERS, EMPLOYERS AND AGENTS, SPECIFICALLY DISCLAIM
ANY IMPLIED WARRANTIES OF TITLE, MERCHANTABILITY, FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR
PURPOSE AND NON-INFRINGEMENT. (Ord. 07-0-78. Passed 8-22-07.)
121.05 DISPOSAL OF SURPLUS AND/OR OBSOLETE PERSONAL PROPERTY.
   (a)   Personal property having a value of more than one thousand dollars
($1,000) but less than five thousand dollars ($5,000) which is surplus and/or
obsolete and no longer needed for a municipal purpose may be sold by the
applicable department director without the necessity of approval of Council and
without utilizing competitive bidding so long as the City Manager, Finance
Director, Law Director and Council President have approved in writing of the
terms of the sale and have agreed with the determination that the property is
surplus and/or obsolete and no longer needed for a municipal purpose.
   (b)   Prior to the delivery of any disposed property, the applicable
department director shall be responsible for the removal of logos, seals,
emblems or other marks identifying the equipment as the property of the City.
   (c)   A list of items sold under this section, the general condition at the
time of sale, the name of the purchaser, the purchase price and the reason for
declaring the item available for sale shall be maintained in the Finance
Director's office and shall be available for public inspection.
   (d)   All monies collected from sale of surplus and/or obsolete personal
property shall be delivered to the Finance Director to be placed in the General
Fund of the City.
(Ord. 15-O-43. Passed 5-5-15.)
121.06 ADOPTION OF ADMINISTRATIVE FEE SCHEDULES.
   When authorized by the Code of Ordinances, the City Manager shall establish
a schedule of administrative fees reasonably calculated to cover the City’s
costs related to the services.
(Ord. 21-O-067. Passed 10-19-21.)
CHAPTER 123
Departments Generally
123.01    Additional departments.
 
CROSS REFERENCES
Authority to establish additional departments - see CHTR.
 3.15
Administrative departments - see CHTR.
 Art. V
 
 
 
123.01 ADDITIONAL DEPARTMENTS.
   For the purpose of administering the offices of the City, in addition to the
departments established under the Charter, there shall be an Engineering and
Building Department, a Department of Planning and Development and a Parks and
Recreation Department, having such powers and duties as provided by law.
(Ord. 18-O-075. Passed 9-4-18.)
CHAPTER 125
Department of Safety
125.01   Powers and duties.
125.02   Rules and regulations of the Police and Fire Departments.
125.03   Additional firefighters.
125.04   Classification of service.
125.05   Emergency medical service rates and regulations.
 
CROSS REFERENCES
Department established - see CHTR.
5.02
Statutory powers and duties - see Ohio R.C. Ch. 737
 
 
 
125.01 POWERS AND DUTIES.
   The Police and Fire Departments shall have the powers, duties and authority
as provided by the Ohio Revised Code and the ordinances of the City, and shall
be operated under the direction of the City Manager, who shall act in the
capacity of Director of Public Safety.
(1969 Code 32.01, 32.04)
125.02 RULES AND REGULATIONS OF THE POLICE AND FIRE DEPARTMENTS.
   The Police Chief and Fire Chief shall make all rules and regulations for the
conduct and discipline of members of their respective departments, which rules
and regulations shall be subject to the approval of the Director of Public
Safety before becoming effective.
(1969 Code 32.07)
125.03 ADDITIONAL FIREFIGHTERS.
   In case of emergency, the Director of Public Safety, or, in his absence, the
Fire Chief may require persons not members of the Fire Department to assist the
Fire Department and be appointed as temporary firefighters for such time as is
deemed necessary during an emergency. (1969 Code 32.08)
125.04 CLASSIFICATION OF SERVICE.
   The Director of Public Safety shall classify the service in the Police and
Fire Departments in conformity with the charter and ordinances of the City, and
shall make all rules for the regulation and discipline of such departments.
(1969 Code 31.09)
125.05 EMERGENCY MEDICAL SERVICE RATES AND REGULATIONS.
   (a)   The City shall provide emergency medical and rescue squad services to
residents of the City and shall bill residents and/or their insurance company,
including the Federal Health Care Programs of Medicare and Medicaid, for the
services provided. Residents shall not pay any insurance co-payment or
deductible amount to the City. The City shall treat the revenues received from
local taxes as payment of any otherwise applicable co-payments and deductibles
due from the residents of the City.
   (b)   Should the City provide emergency medical and rescue squad services to
non- residents of the City, the City shall bill the non-residents and/or their
insurance company including the Federal Health Care Programs of Medicare and
Medicaid for full payment at the rate established by this section.
   (c)   Billing for emergency medical and rescue squad services rendered under
mutual aid shall be as mutually agreed by the involved political subdivisions
and pursuant to paragraphs (a) and (b) hereof.
(Ord. 06-0-119. Passed 10-3-06.)
   (d)   The following schedule of rates shall be charged to individuals who
receive the following services:
 
(1)   Basic Life Support Transportation    $750.00
(2)   Advanced Life Support Transportation
            Category 1            $950.00
            Category 2            $1,150.00
(3)   Loaded Mileage Rate
   Basic Life Support (BLS) Transportation $ 16.00 per mile
   Advanced Life Support (ALS) Transportati$ 16.00 per mile
         (Ord. 19-O-088. Passed 11-6-19.)
   (e)   The City Manager, Fire Chief, and the Director of Finance or their
designees are hereby authorized to establish guidelines and procedures to
assist the indigent and the financially needy that are unable to pay for
emergency medical services.
   
   (f)   All funds collected pursuant to this section shall be disbursed as
follows:
      (1)   Such part thereof as is necessary to defray all expenses of
collection shall be paid.
      (2)   The balance remaining after such payments shall be deposited to the
General Fund.
         (Ord. 06-0-119. Passed 10-3-06.)
CHAPTER 127
Department of Public Works
127.01    Department established.
127.02    Composition.
127.03   Duties of Department.
 
CROSS REFERENCES
Department established - see CHTR.
5.03
Statutory powers and duties - see Ohio R.C. Ch. 735
 
 
 
127.01 DEPARTMENT ESTABLISHED.
   There is hereby established a Department of Public Works which shall be
under the direction of the Director of Public Works who shall be appointed by
and responsible to the City Manager.
(Ord. 08-0-01. Passed 2-5-08.)
127.02 COMPOSITION.
   The Department of Public Works shall consist of one Director of Public
Works, and such additional employees as in the judgment of the City Manager may
be necessary for the efficient operation of the department, but only to the
extent and within the amounts appropriated therefore by City Council.
(Ord. 08-0-01. Passed 2-5-08.)
127.03  DUTIES OF DEPARTMENT.
   The Department of Public Works shall be responsible for the construction and
maintenance of City streets, the maintenance and operation of the stormwater
infrastructure system, the maintenance and operation of the City’s traffic
signal system, the management of the City’s fleet of vehicles and equipment,
the management of the City’s waste collection programs, and the maintenance and
improvements of all municipal public lands, including parks and street trees
and perform such other duties as may be required by the Department Director.
(Ord. 08-0-01. Passed 2-5-08.)
CHAPTER 131
Department of Engineering and Building
131.01    Department established.
131.02    Composition.
131.03    Duties of Department.
131.04    Employing additional engineering help.
 
CROSS REFERENCES
Authority to establish - see CHTR.
3.15
Department established - see ADM.
123.01
 
 
 
131.01 DEPARTMENT ESTABLISHED.
   There is hereby established a Department of Engineering and Building, which
shall be under the direction of the City Engineer and who shall be appointed by
and be responsible to the City Manager.
(Ord. 08-0-01. Passed 2-5-08.)
131.02 COMPOSITION.
   The Department of Engineering and Building shall consist of one City
Engineer, one Chief Building Official and such additional employees as in the
judgment of the City Manager may be necessary to for the efficient operation of
the department, but only to the extent and within the amounts appropriated
therefore by City Council.
(Ord. 08-0-01. Passed 2-5-08.)
131.03 DUTIES OF DEPARTMENT.
   The Department of Engineering and Building shall be responsible to prepare
the necessary surveys, plans and specifications for all engineering projects
carried on in the City. It shall also be responsible for the enforcement of
right of way construction codes, stormwater management codes, erosion and
sediment control codes, building codes, property maintenance codes, and related
planning and zoning codes of the City. It shall also perform such other duties
as may be required by the City Engineer including assisting and cooperating
with the Chief Building Official in the enforcement of all building codes.
(Ord. 08-0-01. Passed 2-5-08.)
131.04 EMPLOYING ADDITIONAL ENGINEERING HELP.
   The City Manager is authorized to employ additional registered professional
engineers to perform services for the City in addition to those performed by
the full time engineer of the City. The services shall include, but not be
limited to, the preparation of all necessary plans, profiles, specifications
and estimates for special projects to be undertaken by the City for the
construction of improvements where a part or all of the cost is to be assessed
upon benefited property.
(Ord. 08-0-01. Passed 2-5-08.)
CHAPTER 135
Department of Finance
135.01    Powers and duties.
135.02    Returned checks.
 
CROSS REFERENCES
Department established - see CHTR.
5.01,
5.04
Statutory powers and duties - see Ohio R.C. Ch. 733
 
 
 
135.01 POWERS AND DUTIES.
   The Director of Finance shall be appointed by the City Manager and shall be
under the supervision and direction of the City Manager except in those cases
or situations where such direction and supervision is or may become in conflict
with the laws of the State of Ohio or the Charter.
   The Director of Finance shall have the powers, duties and authorities
provided by the Revised Code and the Charter and such additional powers, duties
and authority as may be provided by the Council. The Director of Finance shall
devote the necessary time and attention to the affairs of the City as may be
required, and shall have charge of the Department of Finance in the
administration of the financial affairs of the City, including the keeping and
supervision of all accounts, the custody and disbursement of City funds and
monies; the making and collection of special assessments; the issuance of
vouchers and checks, and such other duties as Council may prescribe. The
Director of Finance shall keep accounts showing the financial transactions of
all departments and offices of the City, and shall keep such records and
procedures as to adequately
record all cash receipts and disbursements, all revenues accrued and
liabilities incurred, and all transactions affecting the acquisition, custody
and the disposition of property of the City. All reports required by law shall
be prepared and filed by the Director of Finance.
   The Director of Finance shall prepare such financial reports as required
under State law and by Council. (1969 Code 31.25)
135.02 RETURNED CHECKS.
   The maker of a check payable to the City of Mentor shall be charged the sum
of twenty-five dollars ($25.00) upon said check being dishonored and returned
by the bank due to insufficient funds in the account, closed account, stopped
payment, or any other reason.
(1969 Code 30.36)
CHAPTER 139
Department of Law
139.01   General provisions.
139.02   Law Department established.
139.03   Duties of Law Director.
139.04   Duties of Assistant Law Director, Police Prosecutor.
139.05   Duties of Assistant Law Director.
139.06   Duties of Legal Administrative Assistants and Law Clerk.
139.07   Duties of Legal Administrative Clerk. (Repealed)
 
 
CROSS REFERENCES
         Department established - see CHTR.
5.01,
5.05
         Statutory powers and duties - see Ohio R.C. 733.49 et seq.
 
 
 
139.01 GENERAL PROVISIONS.
   The Law Director shall have the powers, duties and authority provided by the
Charter of the City and Section
139.03.
(1969 Code 31.35)
139.02 LAW DEPARTMENT ESTABLISHED.
     (a)   For the purposes of administering the legal affairs of the City,
there shall be, as provided by the Charter, a Law Department which shall
consist of a Law Director and such assistants as the Law Director shall
designate including an Assistant Law Director-Police Prosecutor, Assistant Law
Director-Zoning Counsel, Legal Administrative Assistants and Law Clerk. At the
discretion of the Law Director any one individual may serve in more than one of
the foregoing capacities.
(Ord. 14-O-95. Passed 10-7-14; Ord. 20-O-024. Passed 3-3-20.)
   (b)   The Law Director, upon written approval of the City Manager and the
Council President, is authorized to appoint and enter into an Hourly Fee
Agreement with temporary assistant law directors for professional services on
an as needed basis in matters including but not limited to conflicts of
interest and temporary staffing.
(Ord. 15-O-74. Passed 9-1-15.)
139.03 DUTIES OF LAW DIRECTOR.
    (a)   The Law Director shall manage and supervise the Law Department;
attend all regular and special Council meetings; prepare legislation, including
but not restricted to legislation required preliminary to and necessary for the
issuance of bonds and/or notes, special assessment proceedings, and note
renewal proceedings, and any other work necessary or incidental to the issuance
of bonds and notes; provide legal representation in behalf of, or in defense
of, the City before all courts, boards, bureaus, commissions, or similar
administrative bodies; counsel and advise the City's officers, boards and
commissions; provide legal representation at the regular and special Zoning
Board of Appeal's meetings and regular and special meetings of the Planning
Commission.
   
   (b)   The Law Director shall maintain office hours at the Mentor City Hall,
or such other places as Council shall designate, at least four (4) different
days each week and not fewer than a total of 15 hours each week. (1969 Code
31.37)
   (c)   The Law Director, upon written approval of the City Manager, is
authorized to adjust and settle any morals claims where the amount to be
disbursed pursuant to the claim does not exceed five thousand dollars ($5,000)
exclusive of administrative costs, subject to a ten thousand dollar ($10,000)
per year maximum in morals claims unless otherwise approved by Council.
(Ord. 15-O-74. Passed 9-1-15.)
139.04 DUTIES OF ASSISTANT LAW DIRECTOR, POLICE PROSECUTOR.
    The Assistant Law Director shall be appointed by the Law Director and shall
perform the duties assigned by and under the general supervision and direction
of the Law Director; perform all the duties of the office of the City Police
Prosecutor and shall prosecute violations of City ordinances where ordinances
provide for a penalty upon conviction.
   The Assistant Law Director shall maintain office hours at the Mentor City
Hall, or such other place as Council may direct and designate, not less than
one (1) day each work week, and not less than six (6) hours each week. This
shall be in addition to court appearances.
(1969 Code 31.38)
139.05 DUTIES OF ASSISTANT LAW DIRECTOR.
   The Assistant Law Director shall be appointed by the Law Director and
perform the duties assigned by and under the general supervision and direction
of the Law Director; attend meetings of the City's boards and commissions as
assigned by the Law Director; and shall prosecute violations of City ordinances
and state statutes where such laws provide for a penalty upon conviction, as
assigned by the Law Director. The Assistant Law Director shall maintain office
hours at the Mentor City Hall, or such other place as Council may direct and
designate not less than one day each work week and not less than six hours each
week. This shall be in addition to court appearances and meeting attendance.
(Ord. 18-O-094. Passed 11-5-18.)
139.06 DUTIES OF LEGAL ADMINISTRATIVE ASSISTANTS AND LAW CLERK.
   The Legal Administrative Assistants and Law Clerk shall perform the duties
assigned by and under the general supervision and direction of the Law
Director; maintain office hours at Mentor City Hall, or such other place as
Council shall designate, during hours to be determined by the Law Director.
(Ord. 14-O-95. Passed 10-7-14.)
139.07 DUTIES OF LEGAL ADMINISTRATIVE CLERK. (REPEALED)
   (EDITOR’S NOTE: Former Section
139.07 was repealed by Ordinance 20-O-024, passed March 3, 2020.)
CHAPTER 143
Department of Parks, Recreation and Public Facilities
143.01    Department established.
143.02   Composition.
143.03    Duties of Department.
143.04    Park and recreation charges and fees.
 
CROSS REFERENCES
Authority to establish - see CHTR.
3.15
 
 
 
143.01 DEPARTMENT ESTABLISHED.
   There is hereby established a Department of Parks, Recreation and Public
Facilities, which shall be under the direction of the Director of Parks,
Recreation and Public Facilities and who shall be appointed by and responsible
to the City Manager.
(Ord. 08-0-01. Passed 2-5-08.)
143.02 COMPOSITION.
   The Department of Parks, Recreation and Public Facilities shall consist of
one Director of Parks, Recreation and Public Facilities, and such additional
employees as in the judgment of the City Manager may be necessary to the
efficient operation of the Department, but only to the extent and within the
amounts appropriated therefore by City Council.
(Ord. 08-0-01. Passed 2-5-08.)
143.03 DUTIES OF DEPARTMENT.
   The Department of Parks, Recreation and Public Facilities shall be
responsible for the maintenance and operation of City-owned buildings, the City
cemetery, and recreation facilities, which are now, or shall in the future,
become the responsibility of the City of Mentor and perform such other duties
as may be required by the Department Director. Said Department shall also be
responsible for such recreation and leisure services programs as shall be
approved by the City Manager within the amounts appropriated therefore by City
Council.
(Ord. 08-0-01. Passed 2-5-08.)
143.04 PARK AND RECREATION CHARGES AND FEES.
   The City Manager shall have the authority to establish charges and fees for
usage of public parks and recreational facilities, and charges and fees
relative to recreational programs.
(Ord. 08-0-01. Passed 2-5-08.)
CHAPTER 147
Department of Planning and Development
147.01   Department established.
147.02   Duties of Department Divisions.
147.03    Composition.
 
CROSS REFERENCES
Authority to establish - see CHTR.
3.15
 
 
 
147.01 DEPARTMENT ESTABLISHED.
   There is hereby established a Department of Planning and Development which
shall have two (2) Divisions. One shall be the Planning, Zoning and Code
Enforcement Division. The other shall be the Economic Development Division.
Each Division shall be under the direction of a Director who shall be appointed
by and responsible to the City Manager.
(Ord. 18-O-075. Passed 9-4-18.)
147.02 DUTIES OF DEPARTMENT DIVISIONS.
   (a)   The Planning, Zoning and Code Enforcement Division shall administer
the following areas, to wit: the Zoning Code including the Subdivision
Regulations: Building Code Enforcement not under the jurisdiction of the Chief
Building Official and the Community Block Grant Program: The Planning, Zoning
and Code Enforcement Division shall also provide assistance to the Planning
Commission, provide assistance to the Board of Building and Zoning Appeals,
prepare long range plans for the growth and development of the City and perform
such other duties as deemed appropriate by the City Manager.
   (b)   The Economic Development Division shall coordinate all economic
development activities of the City and shall perform such other duties as
deemed appropriate by the City Manager.
(Ord. 18-O-075. Passed 9-4-18.)
147.03 COMPOSITION.
   The Department of Planning and Development shall consist of a Director of
Planning and a Director of Economic Development, and such other employees as
the City Manager deems necessary to perform the duties of the Department and
Divisions.
(Ord. 18-O-075. Passed 9-4-18.)
